From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Domenico D'Arrigo <d.darrigo@outlook.it>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 13:57:11 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] drivers/smmstore: End the commit record of a reclaim with a
 write, not an erase

A reclaim of the variable store in SMM erased the commit block when the
copy was done, to clear the record that tells the next boot to finish
the copy. An erase is the slow operation of a flash: one of 64 KiB takes
about a second on the two chips of a ThinkPad W541, where the reclaim
stops the OS for some eight seconds, seven of them in erases (three of
the staging area by the payload, three of the store and this one).

Programming a flash clears bits without an erase, so a record can be
ended by a write of zeros over its magic. Give the record slots of 64
bytes in the first 2 KiB of the commit block, used in order: a reclaim
writes its record into the first slot after which the block is erased,
and ends it there. A slot that is neither erased nor a record is one
that ended. ramstage, which looked for a record at the start of the
block, looks in every slot.

The block is erased when its slots are used up. To keep that erase out
of the reclaims too, ramstage erases the block at boot, where nothing
waits for it, when fewer slots are free than the reclaims of a boot can
use (four); a boot in 28 reclaims at most, and none while the store
does not fill. It erases only a block that holds nothing but records
that ended: the block is also the last one of the spare area of the
payload's fault tolerant write, which leaves a copy of its working
block there and needs it to finish a write that a power loss cut. A
reclaim that still finds no slot, or finds a live record (a copy that
failed in the same boot) or that copy of the payload's, erases the
block itself, as it did.

The notes of the refusals, after the slots of the records, become a log
in the same way: one slot of 32 bytes each, the next free one found by
bisection, the last one the latest. One note at a fixed place would stay
until the next erase of the block, which now is rare, and hide the ones
after it.

An older build reads a record at the start of the block only. If a power
loss cuts a copy whose record is in a later slot and vboot then falls
back to a slot with an older build, that build does not finish the copy
and the payload starts from a damaged store, which it formats; the
record stays, and this code finishes the copy when it boots, if the
staging area is still the image. A record ended by this code is nothing
to the older one, which erases the block before it writes its own.

TEST=tests/drivers/smmstore_reclaim-test: the record ended without an
     erase, the slots over 96 boots with one to four reclaims each and
     the erase by ramstage, the erase by the reclaim when no boot comes
     in between, a block that the payload wrote, a power loss with the
     record in the third slot, the log of the notes and its end.

